// What is left of the standard collections over an element that owns something.
//
// Three classes stand behind `Vector<T>`, and all three used to move elements
// by reading a slot and storing what they read -- and a handle read out of a
// slot is a second handle to one object. `Vector` and `VectorIterator` say the
// weaker thing now: a snapshot is `sub T[]`, `first`, `last` and `toArray`
// hand back the subscriber form, and at `T = int32` none of those qualifiers
// are there at all.
//
// `VectorBuilder` is the one that cannot be fixed by saying something weaker,
// which is why it is recorded here rather than patched. It is a staging owner:
// `add(T)` gives it an element and `finish()` hands every element to a new
// `Vector<T>` that owns them. But it is constructed from a live vector's
// storage, so for an owning element type its first act is to duplicate what
// that vector still holds. Borrowing instead does not help -- `finish()` would
// then give a vector of owners a set of handles it never owned. Whether
// `builder()` should drain the vector, be refused for owning elements, or not
// exist is a decision about what the method means, not a patch.
//
// It is reached here without being called: `Vector<Cell*>` instantiates the
// return type of `builder()` whether or not any line uses it.
//
// `Vector<int32>` is unaffected, and the rest of the suite uses exactly that.
// The corresponding container written the way the model requires is
// tests/vector-owner-elements.abs.

import "../std/collections/vector.abs";

class Cell {
    public int32 value;
    public Cell(int32 initial) { value = initial; }
}

int32 main() {
    Vector<int32>* counted = new Vector<int32>();
    counted.push(1);
    delete counted;

    Vector<Cell*>* owning = new Vector<Cell*>();
    delete owning;
    return 0;
}
